---
import { getRelativeLocaleUrl } from 'astro:i18n';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PartsTable from '../../components/PartsTable.astro';
import { useTranslations } from '../../i18n/utils';
import type { Locale } from '../../i18n/ui';
import '../../styles/build.css';

const locale: Locale = 'en';
const t = useTranslations(locale);
const guideHref = `${getRelativeLocaleUrl(locale, '/')}#guide-heading`;
const coursesHref = getRelativeLocaleUrl(locale, '/courses');
---

<BaseLayout title="Build · Roomie" description="Step-by-step tutorial for building your own roomie." locale={locale}>
	<header class="page-head">
		<h1>{t('build.title')}</h1>
		<p class="page-sub">{t('build.sub')}</p>
	</header>

	<section aria-labelledby="start-heading">
		<h2 id="start-heading" class="section-title">Let's go!</h2>
		<div class="card card--cream">
			<p>
				Let's build your roomie system together! Once you have all the parts, building one roomie unit takes about
				2&nbsp;hours. You need at least two roomie units to use it, so you'll go through these instructions at least
				twice. roomie works with any number of units – so you can build a whole roomie family!
			</p>
			<h3>What to expect when building roomie</h3>
			<ul class="checklist">
				<li>You'll work with a screwdriver</li>
				<li>You'll solder wires with a soldering iron</li>
				<li>You'll drill holes into a plastic enclosure (or: 3D-print the finished enclosure yourself!)</li>
				<li>You'll install and run programs from the command line</li>
				<li>You'll search the internet for great sounds and store them on your roomie</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="hint">
				If you've never assembled electronic components before, ask someone experienced to help you!
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="parts-heading">
		<h2 id="parts-heading" class="section-title">The parts you need</h2>
		<div class="card card--mint">
			<p>
				You can order all the parts for roomie online. While developing roomie, I made sure to use parts that are
				inexpensive and will stay available for a long time. The online shops listed for each component are just my
				recommendations – feel free to order the parts wherever they're available and affordable.
			</p>

			<h3>Bill of materials</h3>
			<p>Each roomie unit is made of the following parts.</p>
			<PartsTable locale={locale} kind="parts" />
			<p>
				Download this parts list as a text file, so you can get the parts step by step and check that you have
				everything.
			</p>
			<div class="actions">
				<span class="btn" aria-disabled="true" title={t('nav.comingSoon')}>Download parts list</span>
			</div>

			<h3>The roomie board</h3>
			<p>
				This is the heart of roomie: the main board with an ESP32-S3 microcontroller module and a class AB audio
				amplifier for the full-range speaker. Can you tell which component is which?
			</p>
			<div class="placeholder placeholder--board">Image or 3D model of the board coming soon</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="tools-heading">
		<h2 id="tools-heading" class="section-title">The tools you need</h2>
		<div class="card card--sky">
			<p>To build roomie, you'll need the following tools:</p>
			<PartsTable locale={locale} kind="tools" />
		</div>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="steps-heading">
		<h2 id="steps-heading" class="section-title">Step by step</h2>
		<ol class="steps">
			<li>
				<div class="card card--pink">
					<h3>Get all the parts</h3>
					<ol class="grid grid--2">
						<li class="sub">
							<h4>Have the boards manufactured and assembled</h4>
							<p>
								Have the roomie main board and the roomie extension board manufactured and assembled by a PCB
								manufacturer.
							</p>
							<div class="placeholder">Text coming soon</div>
						</li>
						<li class="sub">
							<h4>Buy the remaining parts</h4>
							<p>Order all the other parts from the parts list from the online shops of your choice.</p>
							<div class="placeholder">Text coming soon</div>
						</li>
					</ol>
					<p class="hint tape">
						For the small parts in the list above (highlighted in orange), get in touch with me: if I still have
						them in stock, I'll happily send them to you!
					</p>
				</div>
			</li>

			<li>
				<div class="card card--lavender">
					<h3>Prepare your tools</h3>
					<ul class="grid grid--4">
						{
							[1, 2, 3, 4].map((n) => (
								<li>
									<figure>
										<div class="placeholder placeholder--photo">Image {n}</div>
										<figcaption>Short text coming soon</figcaption>
									</figure>
								</li>
							))
						}
					</ul>
				</div>
			</li>

			<li>
				<div class="card card--cream">
					<h3>Assembly</h3>
					<div class="grid grid--2">
						<div class="sub">
							<h4>Option A: Build with a 3D-printed enclosure</h4>
							<p>
								For this option I'm assuming you know your way around 3D printing and have a 3D printer at hand.
								Download the print files below and print the enclosure yourself!
							</p>
							<p>Then continue with option B and skip the steps for working on the enclosure.</p>
							<div class="actions">
								<span class="btn" aria-disabled="true" title={t('nav.comingSoon')}>
									Download print files (.zip)
								</span>
							</div>
						</div>
						<div class="sub">
							<h4>Option B: Build with a store-bought enclosure</h4>
							<p>
								Once you have all the parts, we can start with the actual assembly. You now have two options: either
								follow the guided online instructions and the assembly video, or print out the attached PDF and
								continue offline from here. Your choice.
							</p>
							<div class="actions">
								<span class="btn" aria-disabled="true" title={t('nav.comingSoon')}>
									Online instructions with video ↗
								</span>
								<span class="btn" aria-disabled="true" title={t('nav.comingSoon')}>
									View and print instructions
								</span>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</li>

			<li>
				<div class="card card--mint">
					<h3>Set up roomie</h3>
					<p>
						Now that your roomie units are fully assembled, it's time to bring them to life! This step has three
						parts:
					</p>
					<ol class="grid grid--3">
						<li class="sub">
							<h4>Download the roomie assistant</h4>
							<p>
								Download the roomie assistant as a .zip file from the GitHub repository linked under
								Dev&nbsp;›&nbsp;GitHub.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li class="sub">
							<h4>Install the roomie assistant</h4>
							<div class="placeholder">Text coming soon</div>
						</li>
						<li class="sub">
							<h4>Start the roomie assistant</h4>
							<div class="placeholder">Text coming soon</div>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</div>
			</li>
		</ol>
	</section>

	<section aria-labelledby="done-heading">
		<h2 id="done-heading" class="section-title">Done!</h2>
		<div class="card card--sky">
			<p>
				Great! Your roomie unit is built. If you've only built one roomie so far, repeat these steps for your second,
				third or even fourth roomie! :-) Get roomie up and running by following the instructions in
				the <a href={guideHref}>comic quick setup guide</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				If you'd like to learn more about professional firmware development, or even develop roomie further yourself,
				take a look at the <a href={coursesHref}>Courses</a> on this website.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<p class="ai-note">
		Notes on the use of AI for this page: the German original was written by me, with language corrections made
		using Claude Code. This English version was translated with Claude Code.
	</p>
</BaseLayout>
